          %h1#bondalorlov_reconstruction_theorem Bondal-Orlov reconstruction theorem
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statement"} Statement
              %li
                %a{:href => "#sketch_of_proof"} Sketch of proof
              %li
                %a{:href => "#see_also"} See also
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Thomason and Balmer showed that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories+of+sheaves"} derived category of coherent sheaves
            on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+variety"} smooth variety
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            (i.e. with the tensor product) in addition to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            structure (i.e. as a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+triangulated+category"} tensor triangulated category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+of+a+triangulated+category"} spectrum of a triangulated category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            and similar phenomena. In fact, Bondal and Orlov show how to reconstruct a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} variety
            from its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories+of+sheaves"} derived category of coherent sheaves
            when its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+sheaf"} canonical sheaf
            is ample or anti-ample, using only the graded structure (i.e. the translation functor).
          %h2#statement Statement
          .num_theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a smooth projective variety and suppose that its canonical sheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_X
              is ample or anti-ample. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is another smooth projective variety such that there is an equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D^b(X) \stackrel{\sim}{\to} D^b(Y)
              between the bounded derived categories of coherent sheaves on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \simeq Y
          %h2#sketch_of_proof Sketch of proof
          %p The idea of the proof is to characterize “cohomologically” the closed points, invertible sheaves, and Zariski topology.
          %p
            The main tool we use is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+functor"} Serre functor
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Grothendieck-Serre duality
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Grothendieck-Serre+duality"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(X)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(Y)
            have Serre functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_X = (\cdot \otimes \omega_X)[n]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi Y
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_Y = (\cdot \otimes \omega_Y)[m]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            being the dimensions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            respectively.
          %p
            For simplicity assume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_X
            is ample; the anti-ample case is analogous.
          %p
            %strong Step 1.
            First we characterize the closed points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} k-linear
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  graded category
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graded+category"} ?
              %strong point object
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              satisfying
            %ul
              %li
                (PO-1)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(P) \simeq P[n]
              %li
                (PO-2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom^i(P, P) = 0
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \lt 0
              %li
                (PO-3)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(P) := \Hom(P, P)
                is a field.
          %p
            It is clear that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skyscraper+sheaf"} skyscraper sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sky
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_x = \mathrm{Sky}_x(k(x))
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/residue+field"} residue field
            at any closed point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            is a point object of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_x[r]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_X
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_X
              is ample, then a complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}^\bullet
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(X)
              is a point object iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo •
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}^\bullet \simeq \mathcal{O}_x[r]
              for some closed point
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            In fact, even though we don’t know that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_Y
            is also ample, we get the same result for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be the inverse equivalence to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            preserve point objects. Suppose there was a point object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(Y)
            not isomorphic to any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_y[s]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(P) \simeq \mathcal{O}_{x_0}[r]
            for some closed
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi r
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom(P, \mathcal{O}_y) = \Hom(G(P), G(\mathcal{O}_y)) = \Hom(\mathcal{O}_{x_0}[r], \mathcal{O}_x[r']) = 0
            since
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0 \ne x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mathcal{O}_y : y \in Y\}
            form a
            %span.newWikiWord
              spanning class
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/spanning+class"} ?
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(Y)
            which implies that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = 0
          %p
            %strong Step 2.
            Next we characterize the invertible sheaves on both varieties.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %strong invertible object
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              satisfying for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in \mathbb{Z}
            %ul
              %li
                (IO-1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom^s(L, P) = k(P)
                for all point objects
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %li
                (IO-2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom^i(L, P) = 0
                for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ≠
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \ne s
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If all point objects of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(X)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(X)
              are translations of invertible sheaves on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This means that the invertible objects of both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
